# SHA256 hashes for vendored Ansible collections.
# These were computed from Galaxy downloads (galaxy.ansible.com) at the time of initial vendoring.
# Galaxy does not publish platform-level GPG or Sigstore signatures for community collections,
# so no upstream signature verification was possible at download time.
#
# SHA256SUMS.asc Signature Verification
# ======================================
# SHA256SUMS is signed with RSA key fingerprint AE97E86A1C807F5FA6A7987B68B6396B4E11D882
# To verify the signature:
#   1. Import the public key from the configured keyserver (set in ~/.gnupg/gpg.conf)
#   2. Verify the fingerprint matches: AE97E86A1C807F5FA6A7987B68B6396B4E11D882
#   3. Verify the signature: gpg --verify SHA256SUMS.asc SHA256SUMS
#
# If GPG returns "Can't check signature: No public key", the key is not yet imported.
# Obtain it from a trusted source and import with: gpg --import <key-file>
# Then re-run the verification command above.
#
# SECURITY: Maintainer Identity Verification & Mitigations
# =========================================================
# Galaxy does not publish GPG signatures, Sigstore certificates, or SLSA provenance for community
# collections. If a collection maintainer's Galaxy account is compromised (phishing, credential
# theft, 2FA bypass), attacker can publish malicious versions with valid API hashes, defeating
# hash-based verification. Both the CDN tarball and Galaxy API artifact.sha256 are controlled
# by the same galaxy.ansible.com infrastructure.
#
# Mitigations Implemented (defense-in-depth):
#   1. VERSION PINNING: All collections pinned in requirements.yml (no auto-updates)
#   2. MANDATORY CODE REVIEW: CHANGELOG + new tasks reviewed before committing
#   3. CI INTEGRITY: Pre-commit hook, collections-integrity CI job, bootstrap verification
#   4. GALAXY API CROSS-VERIFICATION: Hash verified against separate Galaxy API service path
#   5. AUDIT TRAIL: Tarballs committed to git (not gitignored) for forensic analysis
#
# See SECURITY.md § "Ansible Collections Supply Chain" for complete vulnerability details,
# attack scenario, and code review procedure.
# See CLAUDE.md § "Ansible Galaxy Collections" for mandatory upgrade checklist.
#
# KNOWN VULNERABILITY (Galaxy account compromise):
# If Galaxy maintainer credentials are compromised, attacker can deploy malicious code that
# defeats hash verification (attacker controls both tarball and API hash). MITIGATION:
# Version pinning + mandatory code review. Report compromises: https://galaxy.ansible.com/security
#
# Python Manifest Verification:
# ==============================
# PYTHON_MANIFEST.json records all Python modules, module_utils, and roles from each
# collection tarball at the time of vendoring. The verify-collections.sh script compares
# extracted collection content against this baseline to detect:
#   - Transitive Python code additions (new modules/plugins not in the original tarball)
#   - Modified Python files (content tampering after extraction)
#
# Methodology: After SHA256 tarball verification, verify-collections.sh:
#   1. Re-extracts all verified tarballs to a temporary directory
#   2. Compares extracted Python files (plugins/*.py, module_utils/*.py, roles/*/*/files/*.py)
#   3. Detects additions, modifications, or removals vs. baseline
#   4. Fails with TOCTOU details if any mismatch found
#
# When bumping a collection version:
#   1. Run make vendor-collections (downloads new tarball, verifies via Galaxy API)
#   2. Run: scripts/gen-collection-manifest.py collections-dist collections-dist/PYTHON_MANIFEST.json
#   3. Review manifest diff: git diff collections-dist/PYTHON_MANIFEST.json
#   4. Ensure only expected modules added (see CHANGELOG)
#   5. Commit requirements.yml, PYTHON_MANIFEST.json, and SHA256SUMS together
#
